---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';

const page = {
  "title": "Six Outcomes, One Stable Answer",
  "description": "Multi-outcome binary meta-analysis with sensitivity diagnostics and subgroup analysis.",
  "field": "Vascular Surgery",
  "venue": "Under Peer Review",
  "year": "2026",
  "status": "Under Review - Details Masked",
  "hook": "Two surgical approaches to the same condition, six outcomes to compare them on, and heterogeneity above 80 percent on the outcomes that mattered most.",
  "scenario": "This one is still under peer review, so the condition and interventions stay masked here. What I can show is the part that made it work: a sensitivity framework built to find out whether the pooled answers were real or the property of one or two studies.",
  "flow": [
    {
      "k": "Clinical question",
      "v": "Between two approaches, which carries less harm across six binary outcomes?"
    },
    {
      "k": "The evidence",
      "v": "Comparative observational studies across multiple countries, some outcomes wildly heterogeneous"
    },
    {
      "k": "The design",
      "v": "Random-effects models wrapped in a four-layer sensitivity framework"
    },
    {
      "k": "The answer",
      "v": "Pooled estimates whose stability is demonstrated, not assumed; manuscript under review"
    }
  ],
  "decisions": [
    {
      "d": "Hartung-Knapp intervals on every outcome.",
      "w": "With modest study counts, the conventional intervals are too confident. Hartung-Knapp pays the honesty tax up front."
    },
    {
      "d": "Leave-one-out refits for all six outcomes.",
      "w": "Each pooled estimate was recomputed dropping every study in turn. If a conclusion dies when one study leaves, that is worth knowing before a reviewer finds it."
    },
    {
      "d": "Baujat plots to name the heterogeneity.",
      "w": "When I-squared passes 80 percent, the useful question is which studies are responsible. The Baujat plot points at them directly."
    },
    {
      "d": "Country subgroups with formal interaction tests.",
      "w": "Practice differs across health systems. Subgrouping with an interaction test checks whether geography explains the disagreement or just relabels it."
    }
  ],
  "problem": "Six binary outcomes comparing two surgical approaches, with extreme heterogeneity on the ones that mattered.",
  "approach": "DerSimonian-Laird random effects with Hartung-Knapp, leave-one-out, Baujat influence, and country subgroups.",
  "outcome": "Under peer review. The framework, six outcomes each carrying their own stability evidence, is the part this page exists to show.",
  "figures": [
    {
      "src": "/assets/projects/tevar/plots/baujat.png",
      "caption": "Illustrative figure from simulated data; the real analysis stays with the journal and the clinical team."
    }
  ],
  "code": [
    {
      "id": "code-tevar",
      "label": "The stability framework",
      "code": "# Random effects with Hartung-Knapp, per outcome\nm <- metabin(event.e, n.e, event.c, n.c,\n             studlab = study, sm = \"OR\",\n             method.tau = \"DL\",\n             method.random.ci = \"HK\",\n             incr = 0.5, allstudies = TRUE)\n\nmetainf(m)          # leave-one-out stability\nbaujat(m)           # who drives the heterogeneity?\n\n# Subgroups with an interaction test\nm_geo <- update(m, subgroup = country)\nm_geo$pval.Q.b.random   # does geography explain it?"
    }
  ],
  "prev": {
    "slug": "seer-cns",
    "title": "The Brain Tumor After the Blood Cancer"
  },
  "next": {
    "slug": "disconnected-nma",
    "title": "The Network That Wasn't Connected"
  }
};
---
<CaseStudyLayout {...page} />
